## Context
Ticket 0050 assigned an RFC 2119 force to every R-item. Its own bound is that
it changes no requirement, threshold or budget: anything reading like a new
ruling goes through DECISIONS.md first. Planning that pass turned up four
sentences that are simply out of date against rulings ratified on 2026-08-29,
and correcting them is a substance change 0050 does not own. They are listed
here so the debt is visible rather than buried in a commit message.

Keywording stale text produces a plausible-looking wrong artifact — a sentence
that now carries contract force and says something the author has already
overruled. That is why 0050 held these back instead of tidying them in passing.

## The four sentences

1. `spec/REQUIREMENTS.md` R20's parenthetical still says the two questions
   "await the author's ruling in DECISIONS.md". Both were ratified 2026-08-29:
   the RSS gate runs in the slow suite with a fast-tier mechanism proxy, and
   the synthetic surrogate satisfies R20's intent conditional on periodic
   revalidation. 0050 already dropped "on every check" from R20's main
   sentence, which the first of those rulings made false; the parenthetical
   below it was left alone.

2. `spec/DESIGN.md` §2.9's RAM paragraph says "It awaits the author's
   ratification, stated once in DECISIONS.md". The per-process scoping was
   ratified the same day.

3. `spec/DESIGN.md` §2.8's R20 gate bullet closes with "ratification is pending
   in DECISIONS.md". Same ruling, same staleness. The "conditional on periodic
   revalidation" clause in that ratification is a textbook RFC 2119 escape and
   should be recorded as SHOULD once the text is corrected.

4. `spec/CONSTRAINTS.md` C3's ratified-budgets list gives "server steady-state
   RSS ≤ ~300 MB" without the per-process scoping the 2026-08-29 entry added.

## Two open questions for the author, not decisions to take

- §2.9's disk budget (≈ 0,8–0,9 GB) carries no keyword. No gate in §2.8 asserts
  it, so MUST would invent a gate and SHOULD would invent an escape the author
  never granted. It reads as an estimate. Confirm, or say which it is.
- C2's constrained-MATCH permission took MAY in 0050. The behaviour is arguably
  owned by DESIGN §2.6 under "one statement per fact", in which case the
  keyword belongs there and C2's line becomes a pointer.

## Also left undone, deliberately

C3's ratified-budgets bullets and the Politeness section state obligations as
bare declaratives with no modal verb, so `bench/check_normative.py` cannot see
them — it scopes to R-items. Giving them keywords is defensible under 0050's
exit criterion ("only obligations take keywords") and was held back because
0050's Action 4 says to leave CONSTRAINTS.md's statements of fact alone. The
same is true of the three rulings above the R-item list in REQUIREMENTS.md,
which carry lowercase "must not" in what are plainly obligations.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] The four sentences say what DECISIONS.md ratified on 2026-08-29
- [ ] The two open questions are answered, in DECISIONS.md if either is a ruling
- [ ] A decision is recorded on whether C3's bare-declarative obligations and
      the three rulings take keywords, and the guard's scope follows it
- [ ] `make check` passes
